}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331821/field-4/d82d724aeba3b5f859cf41443fd7885561318df4b5143d4b9c0e5b00cf33b850" :answer/id #uuid "51265735-918c-5347-a795-3564dc7a69f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331821/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "57d77d53-d269-5f88-9ace-02ff23ed8320" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-331821/field-4/29da04ccd83301c9db11ee3cfb3603fa0c6ba9c5c17cc77fca46a2d11ca22153" :answer-field/choices ["q-331821/field-4/29da04ccd83301c9db11ee3cfb3603fa0c6ba9c5c17cc77fca46a2d11ca22153" "q-331821/field-4/48b411620ed377ee9222b817c8ed23aa5c39bdb48880d81d507d29eeec3ddf84" "q-331821/field-4/d82d724aeba3b5f859cf41443fd7885561318df4b5143d4b9c0e5b00cf33b850"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331821" :question/id #uuid "276a712f-2260-5901-9134-cf67e08a10a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331821" :question/problem "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{b(t)}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{t}b(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t}b(t)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{t}b(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-1)t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $B(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-1)t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t=B(s-1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-1>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331821/field-1" "q-331821/field-2" "q-331821/field-3" "q-331821/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeb2589b-0135-5bbb-bc91-ec121ee5defd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331821"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-1/ca937226df548da96a889675875489dfe0f00d50208b9dec5d9d3e6bba88bb99" :answer/id #uuid "3d6c989d-d804-5d22-b9a8-daa58cbc1d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-1/9b544d835617b16074f484599e940052dc9e3df611c67aeb7e1a415da4492a0d" :answer/id #uuid "f3d88353-254b-5804-9765-3f06c5094051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.00" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-1/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" :answer/id #uuid "fe399233-0217-54a1-aa3c-a7ec1bd9c0e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.00" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7209cf16-57d4-5900-8bd8-54485eaf5c39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339858/field-1/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" :answer-field/choices ["q-339858/field-1/ca937226df548da96a889675875489dfe0f00d50208b9dec5d9d3e6bba88bb99" "q-339858/field-1/9b544d835617b16074f484599e940052dc9e3df611c67aeb7e1a415da4492a0d" "q-339858/field-1/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-2/8bbd105ad792c13b609b7d219284b05b72d7af0273d33d36cad29ed8f19569d5" :answer/id #uuid "dc817c19-7936-5217-be4f-24910db4b114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.00" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-2/8fe45dedf7d7dad70db06c0169d52d74a93a14f7fd20fa84eb4e19f89fa4bbc7" :answer/id #uuid "86457b7d-a09a-52aa-9441-04e651d3c477" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.92" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-2/9d85e464e558142970c7e64b449e2a6b077c45112d0a386494aaecb3d3786201" :answer/id #uuid "a540a999-b790-5a15-ba66-62430f9a382d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2baf4d-7bfe-5b0d-b84e-5c396d357723" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-339858/field-2/8bbd105ad792c13b609b7d219284b05b72d7af0273d33d36cad29ed8f19569d5" :answer-field/choices ["q-339858/field-2/8bbd105ad792c13b609b7d219284b05b72d7af0273d33d36cad29ed8f19569d5" "q-339858/field-2/8fe45dedf7d7dad70db06c0169d52d74a93a14f7fd20fa84eb4e19f89fa4bbc7" "q-339858/field-2/9d85e464e558142970c7e64b449e2a6b077c45112d0a386494aaecb3d3786201"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339858" :question/id #uuid "78ec1cb4-e132-5dea-aae3-8eefb04366b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339858" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\n-3.11\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =0+1+3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =1+2+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈6.00.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339858/field-1" "q-339858/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-1/677b262ecc660d135b9744bdbee526246f8fe9a889061ea525df51679032b8f5" :answer/id #uuid "62d9170a-0909-55f4-9fe0-6cca2ecac89d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\sin(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7576cd8-8f22-5cce-9814-9f7144ceedc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335106/field-1/677b262ecc660d135b9744bdbee526246f8fe9a889061ea525df51679032b8f5" :answer-field/choices ["q-335106/field-1/677b262ecc660d135b9744bdbee526246f8fe9a889061ea525df51679032b8f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-2/600e8d6b300dc1c10996f02dec8320a7f6f7b75fdf0b3c50802d6ba33811fb11" :answer/id #uuid "b8664c3b-4028-5d8a-9c7a-e321fe3c9f51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\cos(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "75e6032d-0ae0-571c-b9e8-8cc5ba5ac664" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335106/field-2/600e8d6b300dc1c10996f02dec8320a7f6f7b75fdf0b3c50802d6ba33811fb11" :answer-field/choices ["q-335106/field-2/600e8d6b300dc1c10996f02dec8320a7f6f7b75fdf0b3c50802d6ba33811fb11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-3/58026cb55d4a55b0559e063e3a6821e5777b2897d61300d16cb70491c64b387b" :answer/id #uuid "c2eab8c2-c2bc-5d46-b9e4-572bfe41f9cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\cos(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "563475c4-fef0-53f4-8db6-761d2e63e40b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335106/field-3/58026cb55d4a55b0559e063e3a6821e5777b2897d61300d16cb70491c64b387b" :answer-field/choices ["q-335106/field-3/58026cb55d4a55b0559e063e3a6821e5777b2897d61300d16cb70491c64b387b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-4/677b262ecc660d135b9744bdbee526246f8fe9a889061ea525df51679032b8f5" :answer/id #uuid "cb5bb032-f45f-5306-95d1-1b7f25da96b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\sin(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "9994f264-3579-5235-9489-32d7222fd6bc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335106/field-4/677b262ecc660d135b9744bdbee526246f8fe9a889061ea525df51679032b8f5" :answer-field/choices ["q-335106/field-4/677b262ecc660d135b9744bdbee526246f8fe9a889061ea525df51679032b8f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335106" :question/id #uuid "483820ad-e2a8-50f1-b1c0-747038058fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335106" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-335106/field-1" "q-335106/field-2" "q-335106/field-3" "q-335106/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335106"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680/selection/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" :answer/id #uuid "6dff7e30-6fab-5622-a471-b61a02ec28d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer/id #uuid "fcaba1e3-8d6f-5688-92cb-36637a462cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "943afe7b-edb9-57a1-b0c2-9d5dd5b78470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680/selection/3de79bddf6f841c5881cdae443d3aaa683eb5842e99646c5b7e2837e89382703" :answer/id #uuid "38aa436d-e12d-5e03-8146-2ef701f52e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "9996c365-0d05-56ea-a331-af6e3c431505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab48fd5-ec24-58b0-bd1f-9a7e9f062342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333680/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-333680/selection/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" "q-333680/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" "q-333680/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" "q-333680/selection/3de79bddf6f841c5881cdae443d3aaa683eb5842e99646c5b7e2837e89382703" "q-333680/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333680" :question/id #uuid "c1b1d018-d137-50d2-a169-085c20ffb16a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333680" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =2{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=0+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333680/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333680"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334866/field-1/58fa22fc775a14700644cb8963fe80129803fefbdd9270631b260cb83d0c3ed7" :answer/id #uuid "012285a6-7505-56a9-b484-4757fd7fd8ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.002" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334866/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf4f3ab-9d3c-5567-89af-3274f3833c1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334866/field-1/58fa22fc775a14700644cb8963fe80129803fefbdd9270631b260cb83d0c3ed7" :answer-field/choices ["q-334866/field-1/58fa22fc775a14700644cb8963fe80129803fefbdd9270631b260cb83d0c3ed7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334866" :question/id #uuid "47bd1286-4bb0-5bbb-bf70-a47f23ff1393" :question/math-academy-id "q-334866" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.162.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={3}^{4}=81.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.162}{81}=0.002.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334866/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-1/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer/id #uuid "896153ea-298d-55bf-95d3-03d4106a3cc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-1/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" :answer/id #uuid "2de69410-b5d1-5cfc-8823-55205fb2e3c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "74dd1965-c5a7-566d-886b-a992a3738dab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "10bd1518-7e96-5d7f-aa41-930d0b399501" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333880/field-1/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer-field/choices ["q-333880/field-1/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" "q-333880/field-1/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" "q-333880/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-2/2e691efea149f30aa1a647ec9c493c66826ac4a5a682cc545f51eba204fa301a" :answer/id #uuid "dbb59fef-824c-597e-b3b5-0059954d627c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-2/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "a4065782-276f-56aa-82c7-cc9cb7d73992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-2/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "faba975e-d67e-564d-bbd4-cb8317d4e16d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "33586d5d-9b82-5f96-9e7c-bc9a833407c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333880/field-2/2e691efea149f30aa1a647ec9c493c66826ac4a5a682cc545f51eba204fa301a" :answer-field/choices ["q-333880/field-2/2e691efea149f30aa1a647ec9c493c66826ac4a5a682cc545f51eba204fa301a" "q-333880/field-2/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" "q-333880/field-2/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333880" :question/id #uuid "ca2ca0e7-5c63-5820-9acf-d569b97931a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333880" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{4}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333880/field-1" "q-333880/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333880"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799/field-1/f19e6df58422d7fb01cce9bdb84691d7edb0be968afd490c200de57c59561755" :answer/id #uuid "5701f4fd-20a2-5745-9be1-470ab3685959" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0" :answer/id #uuid "934b0463-2042-5756-a99f-afedfc606194" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb277d4-b325-5e84-803f-a204af148ae2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340799/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0" :answer-field/choices ["q-340799/field-1/f19e6df58422d7fb01cce9bdb84691d7edb0be968afd490c200de57c59561755" "q-340799/field-1/323c3707f259ac463f43078adb40da54bd6fbd62183256b458b52b49dc2a9da0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" :answer/id #uuid "76df3d62-0039-54c9-8272-1f23fe29acce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799/field-2/d1f6c915b4e3a1496843bec633b4d1faefcdef0d6c86e2f095279d4c052efb98" :answer/id #uuid "db9231ca-8334-5cba-9a62-3340dc5546a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e190814f-7ee1-50b5-88c5-3f7ae0e05e32" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-340799/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" :answer-field/choices ["q-340799/field-2/ad5bfb9ccc3a6d948c3146ce91a4641b42b6fbd31026ea9a2acd2a98208ac3fa" "q-340799/field-2/d1f6c915b4e3a1496843bec633b4d1faefcdef0d6c86e2f095279d4c052efb98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340799" :question/id #uuid "1e2d9d40-82f9-50ff-9728-6b0838a55384" :question/math-academy-id "q-340799" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340799/field-1" "q-340799/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340799"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
